company|stage|topic|difficulty|question|origin|source_url|follow_up|rubric
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|2|Given an array of integers and a target, how would you find two distinct indices whose values sum to the target?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Hash map of seen values; complement lookup; O(n) time and space.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|2|Given a string containing only parentheses, brackets and braces, determine whether every opening symbol is closed in the correct order.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Stack; matching closing symbol; reject leftover openings.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|3|Given a singly linked list, how can you determine whether it contains a cycle using constant extra space?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Floyd slow and fast pointers; meeting implies cycle.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|3|Given an array containing negative and positive integers, find the maximum sum of a nonempty contiguous subarray.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Kadane recurrence; initialize from first element; O(n).
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|3|Given a sorted array rotated at an unknown pivot with distinct values, find a target in logarithmic time.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Binary search; identify sorted half; narrow bounds.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|4|Given a directed graph of course prerequisites, return a valid course order or explain why none exists.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Topological sort; indegrees or DFS; cycle detection.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|4|Design a cache supporting get and put in constant time with least recently used eviction.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Hash map and doubly linked list; move on access; evict tail.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|3|Given a string, find the length of its longest substring without repeated characters.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Sliding window; last seen indices; O(n).
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|4|Given coin denominations and a target amount, find the minimum number of coins needed or report impossibility.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|DP over amounts; unreachable sentinel; unbounded transitions.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|3|Given a list of closed intervals, merge all overlapping intervals.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Sort by start; extend current end; O(n log n).
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|3|Given an unweighted graph, find the shortest path from vertex A to vertex B.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|BFS; visited set; parent reconstruction.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DSA|4|Numbers arrive one at a time. How would you maintain their median after every insertion?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Two heaps; balance sizes; median from roots.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|SQL|2|Employees(id, name, salary, department_id) stores employee records. Write a query to return the second highest distinct salary.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|DISTINCT with ordering and offset or MAX below maximum; handle missing result.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|SQL|3|Customers(id, name) and Orders(id, customer_id) are tables. Write a query returning customers who have never placed an order.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|NOT EXISTS or LEFT JOIN with null order id.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|SQL|3|Employees(id, department_id, salary) stores salaries. Return employees earning more than their own department average.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Correlated subquery or window AVG partitioned by department.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|SQL|3|Orders(id, customer_id, amount) stores orders. Return customers whose total spending exceeds 10000.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|GROUP BY customer_id; SUM; HAVING.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|SQL|4|Employees(id, department_id, salary) stores salaries. Return the three highest distinct salary ranks in each department, including ties.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|DENSE_RANK partition by department order by salary descending; filter rank.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DBMS|3|Two transactions read the same account balance and both update it. How can a lost update occur and how would you prevent it?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Read-modify-write race; atomic update, locking or serializable isolation.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DBMS|3|A query searches a million-row user table by email. What index would you create and how would you verify it helps?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|B-tree on email; EXPLAIN ANALYZE; selectivity and write overhead.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|DBMS|2|An order table repeats customer addresses in every row. What anomalies can occur and how would normalization help?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Update, insertion and deletion anomalies; separate customer relation and foreign key.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|OOP|2|In Java, when would you choose an interface rather than an abstract class? Give a concrete example.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Contract and multiple interfaces versus shared state and implementation.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|OOP|2|A superclass reference points to a subclass object that overrides a method. Which implementation runs in Java and why?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Runtime dispatch for overridden instance methods.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|OOP|3|How would you design a payment feature supporting cards and UPI without changing checkout code for every new payment method?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Strategy interface; implementations; dependency injection.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|OS|3|Two threads acquire locks A and B in opposite order. Explain how deadlock can occur and how to prevent it.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Circular wait; consistent lock ordering; release or timeout.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|OS|2|What resources are shared by threads in one process, and what resources are private to each thread?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Address space and heap shared; stack and registers private.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|OS|3|A program accesses a virtual page not currently in RAM. What happens next?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Page fault; OS validates access; loads page; updates page table.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Networks|2|Describe what happens from entering an HTTPS URL in a browser until the first response arrives.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|DNS; connection; TLS; HTTP request and response.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Networks|3|Why might a video call use UDP while a file download uses TCP?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Latency versus reliable ordered delivery; application tradeoffs.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Networks|3|A client receives HTTP 502 from a reverse proxy. What would you investigate first?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Upstream availability, connectivity, logs and timeouts.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Java|3|How does Java HashMap handle two unequal keys with the same hash code?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Bucket collision; equals; list/tree bins; resize considerations.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Java|2|Why should a mutable object generally not be used as a HashMap key?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Hash or equality changes break lookup consistency.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Java|3|What is the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions? Give an example of each.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Compile-time handling requirement; IOException versus runtime exceptions.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Python|2|Why does modifying a list assigned to another variable also affect the original variable?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Both references point to same mutable object; copying alternatives.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|Python|3|When would a generator be preferable to returning a list of all results?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Lazy iteration; reduced memory; single-pass tradeoffs.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|JavaScript|3|Why does a promise callback run after synchronous code even if the promise is already resolved?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Event loop; microtask queue; call stack.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|JavaScript|2|What is the difference between let, const and var in JavaScript?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Block versus function scope; reassignment; hoisting and TDZ.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|System Design|4|Design an API that prevents a payment from being charged twice when a client retries after a timeout.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Idempotency key; atomic unique record; replay result.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|System Design|3|A public API must allow at most 100 requests per user per minute. How would you enforce this across multiple servers?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Shared counter or token bucket; atomic updates; expiry; 429.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|System Design|4|Design a URL shortener supporting redirects and expiration. What data would you store and how would you handle collisions?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Unique short key; mapping; expiry; collision retry; caching.
TCS Ninja|TECHNICAL|System Design|3|A background job may fail after partially completing its work. How would you make retries safe?|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Idempotent operations; checkpoints; bounded retries and dead-letter queue.
TCS Ninja|INTRO|Communication|1|Introduce yourself in about one minute, focusing on your education, technical interests and a project you are proud of.|ORIGINAL_PRACTICE||false|Relevant concise introduction; concrete experience; clear structure.
